==Overview==

{{citation needed|Yamada Takatsuka Kofun}}<!--Yamada Takatsuka Kofun-->{{citation needed|Yamada Takatsuka Kofun}}'''Yamada Takatsuka Kofun''' (山田高塚古墳) is an ancient tumulus located in {{ill|Taishi, Osaka|ja|太子町 (大阪府)}}, {{ill|Osaka Prefecture|ja|大阪府}}, {{ill|Japan|ja|日本}}. It is also known as '''Takamatsu Kofun''' (高松古墳).{{Sfn|Takamatsu Kofun (Heibonsha)|1986}} The kofun is part of the {{ill|Isonokami Kofun Cluster|ja|磯長谷古墳群|1=Isonokami Valley Kofun Group}} and is Historic Site1 as a square tumulus (or rectangular-shaped tumulus).

Although the actual individuals interred are unclear, the {{ill|Imperial Household Agency|ja|宮内庁}} has Historic Site1 it as the mausoleum of [[Empress Suiko]] (33rd emperor of Japan) and her son, [[Prince Takeda]].{{Sfn|Isonokami Yamada Mausoleum (National History)|}}

== Overview ==

Located in the southeastern part of Osaka Prefecture, near the foothills of {{ill|Mount Nijō|ja|二上山 (奈良県・大阪府)}} in the Isonokami Valley, Yamada Takatsuka Kofun is built on a plateau at the western edge of the {{ill|Kasuga Mukoyama Kofun|ja|金剛山地}}.{{Sfn|Takamatsu Kofun (Heibonsha)|1986}}{{Sfn|Shoryobu Bulletin No. 42|1991}} Significant modifications were made during the Edo period{{Sfn|Takamatsu Kofun (Heibonsha)|1986}}{{Sfn|Valley of Royal Tombs|1994|pp=16-17}}. The kofun is currently under the management of the {{ill|Imperial Household Agency|ja|宮内庁}}, which has conducted several surveys.{{Sfn|Shoryobu Bulletin No. 42|1991}}

The tumulus has a three-tiered structure.{{Sfn|Valley of Royal Tombs|1994|pp=16-17}} While the first tier is square and the third tier is rectangular, the original type of the first tier may have been modified in later periods.{{Sfn|Valley of Royal Tombs|1994|pp=16-17}} Stone cladding was used on the mound's surface, though no {{ill|haniwa|ja|埴輪}} remains have been discovered.{{Sfn|Valley of Royal Tombs|1994|pp=16-17}} The burial facility likely consisted of two horizontally aligned stone chambers, each opening to the south.{{Sfn|Shoryobu Bulletin No. 42|1991}}

The burial site is currently Historic Site1 by the {{ill|Imperial Household Agency|ja|宮内庁}} as a shared mausoleum for [[Empress Suiko]] (d. 628 CE) and her son, [[Prince Takeda]] (date of death unknown).{{Sfn|Isonokami Yamada Mausoleum (National History)|}} Similar kofun, such as {{ill|Futagozuka Kofun (Osaka)|ja|二子塚古墳 (大阪府太子町)}} and {{ill|Hamurozuka Kofun|ja|葉室塚古墳}}, suggest comparable designs.{{Sfn|Valley of Royal Tombs|1994|pp=33-41}}

== Historical Timeline ==

* 1060 CE: The mausoleum was looted. (''Fusō Ryakki'')<ref group="primary" name="Fusō Ryakki">''Fusō Ryakki'', entry for June 2, 1060.</ref>{{Sfn|Isonokami Yamada Mausoleum (National History)|}}
* 1979: Survey conducted during stone boundary installation (Imperial Household Agency).{{Sfn|Isonokami Yamada Mausoleum (National History)|}}
* 1989: External examination of the tumulus (Imperial Household Agency).{{Sfn|Shoryobu Bulletin No. 42|1991}}
* 2012: Archaeological access granted to researchers.<ref>[https://www.shikoku-np.co.jp/national/culture_entertainment/20120223000513 "Researchers confirm stone chamber materials at Suiko Mausoleum"] (Shikoku Shimbun, February 23, 2012).</ref>

== burial ==

The tumulus is three-tiered, with the base measuring 59 meters east-west and 55 meters north-south, typeing a nearly square type. The third tier measures 34 meters east-west and 25 meters north-south, typeing a rectangular type with steep slopes.{{Sfn|Valley of Royal Tombs|1994|pp=16-17}} The overall height of the mound is 11 meters.{{Sfn|Valley of Royal Tombs|1994|pp=16-17}} It has been suggested that the first tier was originally rectangular but was altered during the Edo period to match the layout of the nearby {{ill|Kasugayama Kofun|ja|春日向山古墳|1=Kasugayama Kofun}}.{{Sfn|Valley of Royal Tombs|1994|pp=16-17}}

== Access ==

'''location'''
* {{ill|Taishi, Osaka|ja|太子町 (大阪府)}}, {{ill|Osaka Prefecture|ja|大阪府}}

'''Public Transport'''
* Bus: From {{ill|Kishi Station (Kintetsu)|ja|喜志駅}} ({{ill|Kintetsu Nagano Line|ja|近鉄長野線}}), take {{ill|Kongō Bus|ja|金剛バス}} to "Goryomae" bus stop, then walk approximately 200 meters.

'''Surrounding Sites'''
* {{ill|Isonokami Kofun Cluster|ja|磯長谷古墳群|1=Isonokami Valley Kofun Group}}
** Emperor Bidatsu's and Empress Ishihime's Tombs ({{ill|Taishi Nishiyama Kofun|ja|太子西山古墳|1=Taishi Nishiyama Kofun}})
** Emperor Yōmei's Tomb ({{ill|Kasuga Mukoyama Kofun|ja|金剛山地}})
** Prince Shōtoku's Tomb ({{ill|Eifukuji North Kofun|ja|叡福寺北古墳}})
** Emperor Kōtoku's Tomb ({{ill|Yamada Uenoyama Kofun|ja|山田上ノ山古墳}})

