The burial site is currently Historic Site1 by the {{ill|Imperial Household Agency|ja|宮内庁|12=draft}} as a shared mausoleum for [[Empress Suiko]] (d. 628 CE) and her son, [[Prince Takeda]] (date of death unknown).{{Sfn|Isonokami Yamada Mausoleum (National History)|}} Similar kofun, such as {{ill|Futagozuka Kofun (Osaka)|ja|二子塚古墳 (大阪府太子町)|12=draft}} and {{ill|Hamurozuka Kofun|ja|葉室塚古墳|12=draft}}, suggest comparable designs.{{Sfn|Valley of Royal Tombs|1994|pp=33-41}}

== Historical Timeline ==

* 1060 CE: The mausoleum was looted. (''Fusō Ryakki'')<ref group="primary" name="Fusō Ryakki">''Fusō Ryakki'', entry for June 2, 1060.</ref>{{Sfn|Isonokami Yamada Mausoleum (National History)|}}
* 1979: Survey conducted during stone boundary installation (Imperial Household Agency).{{Sfn|Isonokami Yamada Mausoleum (National History)|}}
* 1989: External examination of the tumulus (Imperial Household Agency).{{Sfn|Shoryobu Bulletin No. 42|1991}}
* 2012: Archaeological access granted to researchers.<ref>[https://www.shikoku-np.co.jp/national/culture_entertainment/20120223000513 "Researchers confirm stone chamber materials at Suiko Mausoleum"] (Shikoku Shimbun, February 23, 2012).</ref>

== burial ==

The tumulus is three-tiered, with the base measuring 59 meters east-west and 55 meters north-south, typeing a nearly square type. The third tier measures 34 meters east-west and 25 meters north-south, typeing a rectangular type with steep slopes.{{Sfn|Valley of Royal Tombs|1994|pp=16-17}} The overall height of the mound is 11 meters.{{Sfn|Valley of Royal Tombs|1994|pp=16-17}} It has been suggested that the first tier was originally rectangular but was altered during the Edo period to match the layout of the nearby {{ill|Kasugayama Kofun|ja|春日向山古墳|1=Kasugayama Kofun|12=draft}}.{{Sfn|Valley of Royal Tombs|1994|pp=16-17}}

