{{ill|Yamada Takatsuka Kofun|ja|山田高塚古墳|1=Yamada Takatsuka Kofun|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Yamada Takatsuka Kofun|13=User:Immanuelle/Yamada Takatsuka Kofun|qq=}} is a [[Kofun]] in Japan. Specifically, it is a {{ill|Rectangular Kofun|lt=Rectangular Kofun|ja|長方形墳|1=Rectangular Kofun|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Rectangular Kofun|13=User:Immanuelle/Rectangular Kofun|qq=|ja_comment=jawiki link invalid}}. It may alternatively be a {{ill|Square Kofun|lt=square-type|ja|方墳|1=Square Kofun|1=Square Kofun|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Square Kofun|13=User:Immanuelle/Square Kofun|qq=|comment=enwiki is a redirect}} kofun. 

The Imperial Household Agency has proclaimed the {{nihongo|'''{{ill|Yamada Takatsuka Kofun|ja|山田高塚古墳|1=Yamada Takatsuka Kofun|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Yamada Takatsuka Kofun|13=User:Immanuelle/Yamada Takatsuka Kofun|qq=}}'''|山田高塚古墳||}} 200 meters to the west to be her official grave site.<ref name="Shiseki">{{cite book |last1=Isomura |first1=Yukio |title=(国指定史跡事典) National Historic Site Encyclopedia |last2=Sakai |first2=Hideya |date=2012 |publisher=学生社 |isbn=4311750404}}{{in lang|ja}}</ref>

There is a tradition that the [[:en:Futagozuka Kofun (Osaka)|Futagozuka Kofun]] is the true tomb of the 33rd [[:en:Empress Suiko|Empress Suiko]] (died 628 AD) and her son Prince Takeda (died 593? AD), however <ref name="Shiseki" />

==Overview==

'''Yamada Takatsuka Kofun''' (山田高塚古墳) is an ancient tumulus located in [[:en:Taishi, Osaka|Taishi, Osaka]], [[:en:Osaka Prefecture|Osaka Prefecture]], [[:en:Japan|Japan]]. It is also known as '''Takamatsu Kofun''' (高松古墳).{{Sfn|Takamatsu Kofun (Heibonsha)|1986}} The kofun is part of the {{ill|Isonokami Kofun Cluster|ja|磯長谷古墳群|1=Shinagadani Kofun Cluster|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Isonokami Kofun Cluster|13=User:Immanuelle/Shinagadani Kofun Cluster|qq=}} and is Historic Site1 as a square tumulus (or rectangular-shaped tumulus).

Although the actual individuals interred are unclear, the [[:en:Imperial Household Agency|Imperial Household Agency]] has Historic Site1 it as the mausoleum of [[:en:Empress Suiko|Empress Suiko]] (33rd emperor of Japan) and her son, [[:en:Prince Takechi|Prince Takechi]].{{Sfn|Isonokami Yamada Mausoleum (National History)|}}

== Overview ==

Located in the southeastern part of Osaka Prefecture, near the foothills of [[:en:Mount Nijō|Mount Nijō]] in the Isonokami Valley, Yamada Takatsuka Kofun is built on a plateau at the western edge of the [[:en:Kongō Range|Kasuga Mukoyama Kofun]].{{Sfn|Takamatsu Kofun (Heibonsha)|1986}}{{Sfn|Shoryobu Bulletin No. 42|1991}} Significant modifications were made during the Edo period{{Sfn|Takamatsu Kofun (Heibonsha)|1986}}{{Sfn|Valley of Royal Tombs|1994|pp=16-17}}. The kofun is currently under the management of the [[:en:Imperial Household Agency|Imperial Household Agency]], which has conducted several surveys.{{Sfn|Shoryobu Bulletin No. 42|1991}}

The tumulus has a three-tiered structure.{{Sfn|Valley of Royal Tombs|1994|pp=16-17}} While the first tier is square and the third tier is rectangular, the original type of the first tier may have been modified in later periods.{{Sfn|Valley of Royal Tombs|1994|pp=16-17}} Stone cladding was used on the mound's surface, though no [[:en:Haniwa|haniwa]] remains have been discovered.{{Sfn|Valley of Royal Tombs|1994|pp=16-17}} The burial facility likely consisted of two horizontally aligned stone chambers, each opening to the south.{{Sfn|Shoryobu Bulletin No. 42|1991}}

The burial site is currently Historic Site1 by the [[:en:Imperial Household Agency|Imperial Household Agency]] as a shared mausoleum for [[:en:Empress Suiko|Empress Suiko]] (d. 628 CE) and her son, [[:en:Prince Takechi|Prince Takechi]] (date of death unknown).{{Sfn|Isonokami Yamada Mausoleum (National History)|}} Similar kofun, such as [[:en:Futagozuka Kofun (Osaka)|Futagozuka Kofun (Osaka)]] and {{ill|Hamuro Kofun Group|lt=Hamuro Kofun Group|lt=Hamurozuka Kofun|ja|葉室塚古墳|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Hamurozuka Kofun|qq=|ja_comment=fixed jawiki redirect|3=葉室古墳群}}, suggest comparable designs.{{Sfn|Valley of Royal Tombs|1994|pp=33-41}}

== Historical Timeline ==

* 1060 CE: The mausoleum was looted. (''Fusō Ryakki'')<ref group="primary" name="Fusō Ryakki">''Fusō Ryakki'', entry for June 2, 1060.</ref>{{Sfn|Isonokami Yamada Mausoleum (National History)|}}
* 1979: Survey conducted during stone boundary installation (Imperial Household Agency).{{Sfn|Isonokami Yamada Mausoleum (National History)|}}
* 1989: External examination of the tumulus (Imperial Household Agency).{{Sfn|Shoryobu Bulletin No. 42|1991}}
* 2012: Archaeological access granted to researchers.<ref>[https://www.shikoku-np.co.jp/national/culture_entertainment/20120223000513 "Researchers confirm stone chamber materials at Suiko Mausoleum"] (Shikoku Shimbun, February 23, 2012).</ref>

== burial ==

The tumulus is three-tiered, with the base measuring 59 meters east-west and 55 meters north-south, typeing a nearly square type. The third tier measures 34 meters east-west and 25 meters north-south, typeing a rectangular type with steep slopes.{{Sfn|Valley of Royal Tombs|1994|pp=16-17}} The overall height of the mound is 11 meters.{{Sfn|Valley of Royal Tombs|1994|pp=16-17}} It has been suggested that the first tier was originally rectangular but was altered during the Edo period to match the layout of the nearby {{ill|Kasugayama Kofun|ja|春日向山古墳|1=Kasuga Mukaiyama Kofun|2=ja|3=春日向山古墳|4=de|5=Kasuga Mukaiyama Kofun|6=fr|7=Kasuga Mukaiyama Kofun|1=Kasugayama Kofun|1=Kasuga Mukaiyama Kofun|2=ja|3=春日向山古墳|4=de|5=Kasuga Mukaiyama Kofun|6=fr|7=Kasuga Mukaiyama Kofun|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Kasugayama Kofun|13=User:Immanuelle/Kasuga Mukaiyama Kofun|qq=}}.{{Sfn|Valley of Royal Tombs|1994|pp=16-17}}

